targetName": "Antioch, Syria", "context": "Lucian of Antioch conducted much of his scholarly and theological work, including founding his school, in the city of Antioch during the late 3rd and early 4th centuries CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "lucian-of-antioch", "sourceName": "Lucian of Antioch", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "great-persecution", "targetName": "Great Persecution", "context": "Lucian of Antioch suffered martyrdom during the Great Persecution under Emperor Maximinus Daia, being executed in Nicomedia in 312 CE."}], "places": [{"name": "Antioch, Syria", "role": "Center of scholarship"}, {"name": "Nicomedia, Bithynia", "role": "Place of martyrdom"}, {"name": "Edessa, Mesopotamia", "role": "Possible education site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Lucian of Antioch", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "lucian-of-antioch"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q510737", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucian_of_Antioch"], "tags": ["Theologians & Scholars", "doctrine-development", "textual-transmission", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Lucian of Antioch's theological school directly shaped the intellectual landscape of the 4th-century Arian controversy and his biblical recensions profoundly influenced Eastern Christian textual traditions for centuries. His contributions thus had a significant regional and thematic impact on the development of Christian doctrine and scripture.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-23T17:41:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30029890340", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Saint Lucian of Antioch, known as Lucian the Martyr, was a Christian presbyter, theologian, and martyr. He was noted for both his scholarship and ascetic piety.", "newValue": "Lucian of Antioch, a martyred Christian presbyter and theologian, was a pivotal figure in early Syriac Christianity, active in the late 3rd and early 4th centuries CE. Operating from the vibrant intellectual hub of Antioch, he established a renowned theological school that became a wellspring for later Arian and semi-Arian doctrines, profoundly shaping Christological debates for decades. His meticulous textual scholarship on the Greek Septuagint and New Testament also laid groundwork for future biblical criticism, influencing the textual tradition of the Eastern churches.

Educated possibly in Edessa, Lucian later settled in Antioch, where he founded a private theological academy. This school became famous for its literalist exegetical approach, emphasizing a rational and philosophical interpretation of scripture, in contrast to the allegorical methods prevalent in Alexandria. Among his most influential works was a critical revision of the Greek Septuagint, aiming to restore it to a more accurate Hebrew original, which became widely adopted in the Eastern churches. He also produced a recension of the Greek New Testament. During the Great Persecution under Emperor Maximinus Daia, Lucian was arrested in Nicomedia, enduring torture and imprisonment before his execution in 312 CE.

Lucian's enduring legacy is multifaceted. His Antiochene school produced a cadre of influential theologians, including Eusebius of Nicomedia and Arius himself, whose teachings sparked the Arian controversy, a central theological conflict of the 4th century. The Nicene Creed, formulated in 325 CE, was largely a response to the theological currents emanating from Lucian's intellectual lineage. His biblical recensions, though not universally accepted, profoundly influenced textual transmission in the East, providing a standardized text that persisted for centuries, particularly within the Syrian tradition."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-23T17:41:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30029890340", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "240 CE, Samosata"}, {"title": "Theological debates and doctrinal development", "type": "Idea", "year": "240 CE, Samosata"}], "newValue": ["The intellectual ferment of 3rd-century Antioch provided a fertile ground for Lucian's theological school and textual scholarship.", "The ongoing Christological debates between Antioch and Alexandria motivated Lucian's emphasis on literal scriptural interpretation.", "The Great Persecution under Roman emperors Diocletian and Maximinus Daia led to Lucian's arrest, torture, and eventual martyrdom in 312 CE."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-23T17:41:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30029890340", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Developed theological frameworks influencing doctrine", "type": "Idea", "year": "312 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "312 CE"}], "newValue": ["Lucian's Antiochene school directly influenced Arius and Eusebius of Nicomedia, precipitating the Arian controversy in the 4th century.", "His critical recen